.custom = .{ .doc_section = "Learn" },
---
LLMD's telemetry serializer prepares two kinds of events for PostHog's capture
API: `session_started` and `heartbeat`. They describe the running model,
serving configuration and aggregate usage counters. This page describes the
provided serializer; it does not establish when events are sent, the heartbeat
interval, delivery guarantees or telemetry controls.

For the Prometheus metrics exposed at `/metrics`, see
[Scrape service metrics]($link.page('llmd/howto/monitoring-and-troubleshooting').ref('scrape-service-metrics')).

# [Session identity and enrichment]($heading.id('identity-and-enrichment'))

Both event types use the process-scoped session ID as PostHog's `distinct_id`.
The identifier groups events for that process session.

Both set `$process_person_profile` to `false`, disabling person profiles, and
`$geoip_disable` to `false`, enabling GeoIP enrichment. Disabling person
profiles does not disable GeoIP enrichment. The serializer does not show which
geographic properties PostHog adds.

# [Properties on both events]($heading.id('shared-properties'))

Alongside the identity and enrichment settings, both event types include:

| Property | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `schema_version` | Telemetry schema version. |
| `timestamp_unix_ms` | Event timestamp in Unix milliseconds. |
| `llmd_revision` | LLMD revision for the session. |
| `model_type` | Model type for the session. |
| `api_kind` | Kind of API served. |
| `platform_target` | Platform target for the session. |
| `device_count` | Number of devices. |
| `device_kind` | Kind of device. |

# [Session start configuration]($heading.id('session-start'))

The `session_started` event also includes these configuration properties:

| Property | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `configured_backend` | Configured execution backend. |
| `batch_size` | Configured batch size. |
| `token_batch_size` | Configured token batch size. |
| `max_context_length` | Configured maximum context length. |
| `prefill_chunk_size` | Configured prefill chunk size. |
| `page_chunk_size` | Configured KV-cache page chunk size. |
| `speculative_decoding_enabled` | Whether speculative decoding is enabled. |

See [Flags list]($link.page('llmd/reference/flags-list')) for the related launch
options. These configuration properties are included on session start, rather
than repeated in the heartbeat properties shown here.

# [Heartbeat counters]($heading.id('heartbeat-counters'))

The `heartbeat` event includes `uptime_seconds` and the counters below.
Counter names ending in `_total` represent totals, rather than rates. The
serializer copies the counter values; it does not calculate throughput,
latency or averages.

## [Shared counters]($heading.id('shared-counters'))

| Property | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `input_tokens_total` | Total input tokens. |
| `prefill_tokens_total` | Total prefill tokens. |

## [Generation counters]($heading.id('generation-counters'))

For a generation API, the heartbeat includes:

| Property | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `output_tokens_total` | Total output tokens. |
| `requests_finished_eos_total` | Requests finished with an end-of-sequence condition. |
| `requests_finished_length_total` | Requests finished at a length limit. |
| `requests_finished_error_total` | Requests finished with an error. |
| `requests_finished_canceled_total` | Requests finished by cancellation. |
| `requests_rejected_oom_total` | Requests rejected due to an out-of-memory condition. |

## [Embedding counters]($heading.id('embedding-counters'))

For an embedding API, the heartbeat includes:

| Property | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `embedding_inputs_completed_total` | Embedding inputs completed. |
| `embedding_inputs_error_total` | Embedding inputs with an error. |
| `embedding_inputs_canceled_total` | Embedding inputs canceled. |
| `embedding_inputs_rejected_oom_total` | Embedding inputs rejected due to an out-of-memory condition. |

The serializer selects counters for the served API and flattens them into the
heartbeat properties. Counters for the other API are left out: their values
are set to `null` before serialization. Embedding counters count inputs;
generation completion and rejection counters count requests.

# [Payload scope and size]($heading.id('payload-scope'))

The property lists shown in the serializer contain configuration metadata and
aggregate counters. They contain no prompt text, generated text, embedding
vectors, model repository path or request body. This describes these event
properties, rather than other logging or reporting paths.

Serialization uses a fixed buffer bounded by `max_event_size` and can return
`EventTooLarge`. The provided code does not specify that size or how the
caller handles a serialization failure.
